   WASHINGTON, D.C., NOV 06-10, 1999},
Organization = {Amer Med Informat Assoc},
Abstract = {Objective: Describe the perceptions of housestaff physicians about their
   experience using computerized physician order entry (POE) in hospitals.
   Methods: Qualitative study using data from participant observation,
   focus groups, and both formal and informal interviews. Data were
   analyzed by three researchers using a grounded approach to identify
   patterns and themes in the texts.
   Results: Six themes were identified, including housestaff education,
   benefits of POE, problems with POE, feelings about POE, implementation
   strategies, and the future of POE.
   Conclusion: House officers felt that POE assists patient care but may
   undermine education. They found that POE works best when tailored to fit
   local and individual workflow. Implementation strategies should include
   mechanisms for engaging housestaff in the decision process.},
