   17-21, 2003},
Organization = {IEEE Engn Med \& Biol Soc; Coral; Univ Autonome Metropolitana; Sandia
   Natl Lab},
Abstract = {Classifying emotion and emotion related disorders in the voice have
   often been studied utilizing prosodic (pitch, energy, speaking rate) and
   other spectral characteristics (formants, power spectral density) of the
   acoustic speech signal. Glottal waveform features have received little
   attention in the study of many emotion and emotion related disorders,
   but have shown strong correlations in a variety of speech pattern
   studies including speaker characterization and stress analysis. We
   employ glottal extraction techniques to obtain features related to
   timing, ratios, shimmer, and spectral characteristics of the glottal
   waveform in the study of clinical depression. Our study reports on
   several glottal waveform features that show very good separation among a
   control group and patient group of males and females suffering from a
   depressive disorder.},
