Year = {1998},
Pages = {1751-1756},
Note = {4th International Conference on Probabilistic Safety Assessment and
   Management, NEW YORK, NY, SEP 13-18, 1998},
Organization = {Int Assoc Probabilist Safety Assessment \& Management; Amer Inst Chem
   Engineers; Amer Nucl Soc; Amer Soc Civil Engineers; Amer Soc Mech
   Engineers; Atom Energy Soc Japan; European Nucl Soc; European Safety \&
   Reliabil Assoc; Fed Aviat Adm; Human Factors Soc; Inst Elect \& Electr
   Engineers; Int Atom Energy Agcy; European Union, Joint Res Ctr; NASA;
   Org Econ Cooperat \& Dev; Soc Risk Analy; Syst Safety Soc; UK Safety \&
   Reliabil Soc; US Coast Guard; US DOE; US Nucl Regulatory Commiss;
   Associated Univ Inc; Beta Corp Int; BOOZ ALLEN \& HAMILTON Inc;
   Brookhaven Sci Associates; ERIN Engn \& Res Inc; PLG Inc; Sci Applicat
   Int Corp; SCIENTECH INC; Univ Maryland, Ctr Technol Risk Stud},
Abstract = {The study of public perceptions of risk has given rise to a number of
   different (and sometimes conflicting) perspectives. Although the
   differences between these approaches are not trivial, recent reviews
   have suggested that there may be some points of convergence {[}1, 2]. In
   particular, recent work within all three of these traditions has
   emphasised the importance of factors such as trust and power for
   understanding public perceptions of risk. These factors take us beyond
   the characteristics of the risks themselves, which have been the focus
   of influential work in the psychometric tradition {[}3], and into a
   consideration of the social and cultural context within which
   potentially hazardous technologies are encountered and evaluated. In
   this paper we are concerned with the way in which the lay public
   understand and respond to a particular class of technological risks -
   those associated with site-based hazards. On the basis of our research
   we argue that an appreciation of the contexts within which citizens
   encounter such risks is crucial to understanding the dynamics of public
   concern. The following discussion draws upon a recently completed study
   of public perceptions of the risks associated with seven different major
   hazard sites in the UK, which was funded by the Health and Safety
   Executive. The views of citizens living within the designated
   consequence zone around each of these sites were investigated using a
   combination of focus group discussions and Q-sort exercises, together
   with background interviews with key local actors and the collection of
   documentary data on the locality.},
