   DECIS SCI INST},
Title = {A controlled study of watt perception bias in queues},
Booktitle = {DECISION SCIENCES INSTITUTE 1998 PROCEEDINGS, VOLS 1-3},
Year = {1998},
Pages = {1636-1638},
Note = {29th Annual Meeting of the Decision-Sciences-Institute, LAS VEGAS, NV,
   NOV 21-24, 1998},
Organization = {Decis Sci Inst},
Abstract = {A basic characteristic of service businesses is the occurrence of
   customer-provided inventories. Customers provide their selves, their
   belongings, or their information to the service provider for processing.
   The organized delay of customer inventories is a queue. A fundamental
   distinction between traditional manufacturing inventories and
   customer-provided inventories is the psychological waiting costs to the
   customer. With services, perceived delays can be more relevant than
   actual delays. Some researchers, such as Maister, have supposed factors
   which influence perceived queue delays, but Little empirical research
   has been done in this area. This paper describes an attempt to
   empirically test the effect of queue delay information on wait
   perception bias. Results illustrate how difficult empirical studies of
   queuing can be. Suggestions for future research are described.},
