   Univ, Princeton, NJ, NOV 12, 2004},
Organization = {Cooperat Inst Climate Sci; Program Sci, Technol \& Environm Policy},
Abstract = {A national, representative survey of the U.S. public found that
   Americans have moderate climate change risk perceptions, strongly
   support a variety of national and international policies to mitigate
   climate change, and strongly oppose several carbon tax proposals.
   Drawing on the theoretical distinction between analytic and experiential
   decision-making, this study found that American risk perceptions and
   policy support are strongly influenced by experiential factors,
   including affect, imagery, and values, and demonstrates that public
   responses to climate change are influenced by both psychological and
   socio-cultural factors.},
