   Antalya, TURKEY, APR 22-25, 2010},
Abstract = {The general purpose of this study, carried out in Serbia, was to measure
   the effects of neurological illness on the attachment perception.
   Twenty-nine children affected by epilepsy, 36 by cerebral palsy, and 50
   children without any pathology as control group constituted the sampling
   of this study. The data were collected using the Security Scale (Kerns,
   Klepac, and Cole, 1996). The results show statistical differences
   between children with neurological illness and the control group. A
   relation between the kind of treatment (hospitalization versus
   day-hospital) and the level of attachment was found. However, there were
   no differences between the group with epilepsy and the group with
   cerebral palsy. (C) 2010 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.},
